\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic {eventually}-style machine-execution goal past an inlined
modular-multiplication subroutine, replacing the current state variable by a
later one and adding an assumption characterising the newly written result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which of several variants/offsets of the
inlined code block to step over; the second, a {string list}, names the
variable to introduce for the product together with the two input variables it
is formed from. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement
about state {sN}, it yields a single subgoal about a later state variable {sM}:
{
   A, state-facts about sN ?- eventually P sN
   =========================================  LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC n ["t"; "u"; "u'"]
   A, t = (u * u') MOD p_25519, state-facts about sM, read dest sM = t ?- eventually P sM
}
\noindent Here the whole block of state-dependent facts about {sN} —
program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — is discharged and re-added in terms of {sM},
with the frame extended by the locations written and a new read assumption
giving the destination contents as the freshly named variable {t}, for which
the defining equation {t = (u * u') MOD p_25519} is also added. Purely
arithmetic or side-condition assumptions not mentioning the state are left in
place, though some may be removed and re-added unchanged, so their position in
the list shifts.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about an x86
machine state.

\ENDDOC
